For the product rule, insert $f(a) g(a+h)$ and rearrange:
$
  (f(a+h) g(a+h) - f(a) g(a))/(h)
    = (f(a+h) - f(a))/h g(a+h) + f(a) (g(a+h) - g(a))/h.
$
As $h -> 0$, the first factor tends to $f'(a)$; the middle factor tends to $g(a)$ because $g$ is differentiable at $a$ and therefore continuous at $a$; and the last factor tends to $g'(a)$. This gives $(f g)'(a) = f'(a) g(a) + f(a) g'(a)$.

The addition rule follows by splitting the difference quotient of $f + g$ into the difference quotients of $f$ and $g$, and the reciprocal rule is the quotient rule with constant numerator $1$.
